Glenn Gore (Affinidi Pte Ltd): Yes.

468
00:56:05.550 --> 00:56:06.450
Nicky Hickman (Digital Egret Ltd): Go ahead.

469
00:56:07.920 --> 00:56:11.269
Geoff Turk: I was just showing the browser plugin while you guys are talking.

470
00:56:12.390 --> 00:56:21.780
Glenn Gore (Affinidi Pte Ltd): Nicky, you just touched on a really important point, and this is part of the design thesis of what you're seeing, Geoff showing.

471
00:56:22.500 --> 00:56:42.260
Glenn Gore (Affinidi Pte Ltd): There's actually 3 layers you should think of from a design perspective here. There's the VTA and what it's technically capable of, there's trust tasks, which is what you're allowed to change from outside the VTA, and then there's the UX skin on front. And so, if…

472
00:56:42.630 --> 00:56:54.100
Glenn Gore (Affinidi Pte Ltd): the technical representation in the VTA of attributes and faces and personas, and we can change the names of those, by the way, they're just kind of internal names to the VTA.

473
00:56:54.390 --> 00:57:04.319
Glenn Gore (Affinidi Pte Ltd): That then means if a different society, group, a different community, different ethnic group actually have a very different way of how they want to

474
00:57:04.320 --> 00:57:15.040
Glenn Gore (Affinidi Pte Ltd): show identity or have different names for it. That is completely customizable on the UX front. You can do whatever you like with this. Don't, I…

475
00:57:15.180 --> 00:57:28.709
Glenn Gore (Affinidi Pte Ltd): designed this so it's not a one-size-fits-all, because it's a pet hate of mine. If people want to radically change the UX, as long as it fits on the VTA technical construct, you can pretty much do what you like with it.

476
00:57:32.230 --> 00:57:35.869
Nicky Hickman (Digital Egret Ltd): This is just music to our ears, Glenn.

477
00:57:36.040 --> 00:57:45.680
Nicky Hickman (Digital Egret Ltd): Finally, we've got something that enables people to project An identity in context.

478
00:57:46.830 --> 00:57:51.870
Nicky Hickman (Digital Egret Ltd): That they… and to tell their own story, you know, to…

479
00:57:51.970 --> 00:58:01.969
Nicky Hickman (Digital Egret Ltd): to do it for themselves, in a digital world, where you would naturally do it in the real world. When you walk into a room.

480
00:58:01.970 --> 00:58:16.099
Nicky Hickman (Digital Egret Ltd): The way you introduce yourself to each other depends on whether it's a party, or a business conference or, you know, a medical appointment, even if it is the same person that you're talking to.

481
00:58:16.300 --> 00:58:23.659
Nicky Hickman (Digital Egret Ltd): So, we're coming up for the last couple of minutes. Anyone else got anything they'd like to…

482
00:58:23.880 --> 00:58:25.749
Nicky Hickman (Digital Egret Ltd): Add to the conversation.

483
00:58:28.070 --> 00:58:35.230
Margaret Novotny: I just have one clarifying question for Glenn on that. So, it's wonderful to have this

484
00:58:35.980 --> 00:58:41.999
Margaret Novotny: flexibility in the UX layer for, describing identity

485
00:58:43.030 --> 00:58:49.030
Margaret Novotny: So that that can be culturally or contextually, Change as needed.

486
00:58:49.430 --> 00:58:53.209
Margaret Novotny: But that's also kind of a double-edged sword.

487
00:58:54.280 --> 00:59:00.340
Margaret Novotny: In the sense that, who, or… or…

488
00:59:00.980 --> 00:59:03.640
Margaret Novotny: what… at what level is… I mean.

489
00:59:03.840 --> 00:59:13.490
Margaret Novotny: So if I'm in… let's say I'm in Iceland versus I'm in, Antarctica, and these are two culturally different contexts.

490
00:59:14.180 --> 00:59:14.900
Margaret Novotny: Nope.

491
00:59:15.920 --> 00:59:27.940
Margaret Novotny: Is it a… Culture-level decision on how identity is represented, or is it a… Community-level decision, or is it…

492
00:59:28.140 --> 00:59:38.170
Glenn Gore (Affinidi Pte Ltd): Yeah, so it's… your VTA is always you, and so it's your choice as to what

493
00:59:38.580 --> 00:59:51.069
Glenn Gore (Affinidi Pte Ltd): system of identity you want to choose to represent yourself with. And so that's always your choice, no community takes that away from you. And when you join, let's say you are in,

494
00:59:51.310 --> 01:00:07.010
Glenn Gore (Affinidi Pte Ltd): Iceland, and you set yourself up in an Icelandic way, and now you're going to join, I don't know, Greenland, and they have a different way. The VTA and VTC, they're negotiating, they're translating between the two of them. But the way you think and identify yourself stays true to yourself.

495
01:00:07.210 --> 01:00:15.440
Glenn Gore (Affinidi Pte Ltd): And if the VTC ever has a governance or policy that would restrict that, then that's your decision now whether you want to join that community or not.

496
01:00:16.510 --> 01:00:18.440
Margaret Novotny: Got it. Okay, thank you.

497
01:00:20.980 --> 01:00:30.079
Glenn Gore (Affinidi Pte Ltd): I think that's a key point. Always… your VTA is always a representation of yourself. It never gets corrupted by the VTC.

498
01:00:30.150 --> 01:00:46.169
Glenn Gore (Affinidi Pte Ltd): Which is the reverse of how platforms work today. Today's… the platform, which is the VTC, has the power of how it chooses to represent you. You don't get to have control over that. That's the big flip here that we're doing.

499
01:00:46.490 --> 01:00:51.239
Glenn Gore (Affinidi Pte Ltd): You're pushing yourself into the community, not the other way around.

500
01:00:52.070 --> 01:01:01.910
Daniel Glinz (Glinz & Company GmbH): But what if you could benefit from a translator, so that the VDA automatically translates into another cultural area, and you might be fine with that?

501
01:01:01.910 --> 01:01:02.290
Glenn Gore (Affinidi Pte Ltd): That's…

502
01:01:02.370 --> 01:01:04.430
Daniel Glinz (Glinz & Company GmbH): But that could be dangerous, couldn't it?

503
01:01:04.900 --> 01:01:05.470
Daniel Glinz (Glinz & Company GmbH): Yes, sir.

504
01:01:05.470 --> 01:01:08.270
Glenn Gore (Affinidi Pte Ltd): So again, you would… that would be a policy…

505
01:01:08.570 --> 01:01:13.760
Glenn Gore (Affinidi Pte Ltd): part of a community of what they're willing… what they're willing to do, but also what you're willing to do.

506
01:01:13.760 --> 01:01:14.890
Daniel Glinz (Glinz & Company GmbH): Right, right, yeah.

507
01:01:14.890 --> 01:01:21.199
Glenn Gore (Affinidi Pte Ltd): If you both don't agree, nothing's gonna happen. If you both can agree on something, then whatever you've agreed on is what will happen.

508
01:01:25.910 --> 01:01:31.679
Nicky Hickman (Digital Egret Ltd): Fantastic. We're just a couple of minutes over. Thank you all very much for your time.

509
01:01:32.140 --> 01:01:41.830
Nicky Hickman (Digital Egret Ltd): And the amazing work. We're all in awe of you guys beavering away, and hope that we can help.

510
01:01:43.600 --> 01:01:44.330
Nicky Hickman (Digital Egret Ltd): Okay.

511
01:01:44.330 --> 01:01:45.930
Glenn Gore (Affinidi Pte Ltd): Very helpful. Take care.

512
01:01:46.470 --> 01:01:48.109
Nicky Hickman (Digital Egret Ltd): See you guys tomorrow.

513
01:01:48.280 --> 01:01:48.860
Nicky Hickman (Digital Egret Ltd): Bye.

514
